TP.register({id:"lp7-g5",lp:7,grade:"5",mode:"curriculum",checks:6,nextGrade:"6th grade",build(E){const F=E.F;const FLU_TITLE="Ten O'Clock Under the Awning";const FLU=["The library opens its side door at a quarter to ten on the second Saturday of the month, and whoever is first out carries the trestles. There are two of them, and they are heavier than they look, because the legs are solid and the tops are an inch and a half of pine that somebody donated in the first year and nobody has ever complained about.","Setting up takes about twenty minutes. The electrics table goes nearest the door, because it needs the socket. The textiles table goes at the far end, under the part of the awning that does not drip. Between them is a folding chair that is not for a volunteer. It is for whoever is next.","By five past ten there is usually a line. People bring lamps with cracked plugs, bags with torn straps, a kettle, a chair with a loose rung, and once a clarinet, which nobody could fix and everybody wanted to look at.","The last thing to go out is the tally sheet, clipped to a board and hung on a nail by the door. Every item that comes to the table gets a line on it. The sheet is now two inches thick, and nobody has volunteered to type it up."].join("\n\n");const STORY_TITLE="The Second Table";const STORY=["The Westmill repair table runs on the second Saturday of the month, from ten until two, under the awning at the side of the library. There are really two tables. The one by the door is small electrics, which is where everyone wants to sit, because things there either work at the end or they do not, and either way you find out. The other table is textiles, which is zippers and hems and the straps of bags, and which Ines had been assigned to without being asked.","She had put her name down in October, mostly to get out of a Saturday chess club she had stopped liking in September, and had not thought past that.","“You will get electrics in March,” Mr. Wendt said, without looking up. He is ninety-one and does the zipper work with a seam ripper and a pair of pliers and a steadiness Ines could not have faked. “There is a list.”","At twenty past ten on the second Saturday in November, a girl of about seven came under the awning with a canvas bag and a face that said she had been told to wait her turn and did not want to. The strap had torn out of one corner. Mr. Wendt was three jobs deep. Ines looked at the bag, then at the box of heavy thread, then at the girl.","“Do you want to do it,” Ines said, “or do you want me to?”","The girl said she wanted to.","It took forty minutes. Ines threaded the needle eleven times, because the girl kept pulling the thread back out by accident and then apologizing, and by about the fifth time the apologizing stopped, which Ines thought was the actual repair.","At one o'clock Mr. Wendt slid the clipboard down the table. “March,” he said. “Electrics. Put your name in.”","Ines looked at the list for a while. Then she wrote her name in the textiles column, which was shorter, and slid it back."].join("\n\n");const ART_TITLE="What Comes to the Table";const ART_SRC="Text 1: “What Comes to the Table,” by Priyanka Raman. Westmill Register, February 2026.";const ART=["A repair table is a few hours and a few people with tools. Westmill's runs on the second Saturday of the month, ten until two, under the awning at the side of the library, and it has run for 26 months.","The volunteers keep a tally sheet. Over those 26 months, 611 items have come to the table. 388 of them left working that same day. 102 could not be fixed at all and were sent home with a note about where to take them. The other 121 needed a part the table did not have. 44 of those came back the next month with the part and were finished then, so 432 items in all have gone home working.","The tally sheet also records what comes. Small electrics are the largest group at 214 items, and lamps alone account for 96 of those. Textiles are second at 177. Everything else comes to 220: chairs, bicycle brakes, a kettle, a clarinet, one wooden clock.","The table charges nothing and accepts nothing. There is a jar on the end of the table, and it is for the library.","What the tally sheet does not record is the part the volunteers talk about most, which is that people stay. The average visit, timed over one Saturday in November, was 52 minutes, and most of that was waiting."].join("\n\n");const NOTICE_SRC="Text 2: Notice posted by the awning door. Westmill Public Library.";const NOTICE=["The repair table is a volunteer table. The library lends the awning, two trestles, and the power. Please read all five rules before you join the line.","1. One item per person per Saturday. If the table is quiet at one o'clock, bring a second.","2. Stay with your item. The volunteers will not work on anything left behind.","3. Nothing is guaranteed. A volunteer may open a thing and tell you it cannot be fixed.","4. The jar on the end of the table is for the library. The repair table does not take money.","5. Children are welcome at the table and may work on their own item with a volunteer beside them."].join("\n\n");const LETTER_SRC="Text 3: Letter to the Westmill Register from Aurelio Wendt, repair table volunteer. February 2026.";const LETTER=["Your article counted what we mended. I would like to say something about what we did not mend.","I have sat at the textiles table for 26 months. The number that matters to me is not 432. It is rule 2 on the library's notice: stay with your item. We wrote that rule because the first six months were people dropping bags off, and a drop-off table is a free repair shop, which is a fine thing, but it is not what we are.","The reason to stay is that you watch. Most people who watch a zipper get fixed twice can fix the third one themselves. In November a seven-year-old sewed a bag strap at my table over forty minutes, with one of our newer volunteers threading the needle for her, and neither of us touched the bag.","I will add that the waiting is not a problem to be solved. Fifty-two minutes is how long it takes two neighbors to find out they live on the same street."].join("\n\n");const MINUTES_READ=["Minutes of the Westmill repair table volunteers, November meeting.","Eleven volunteers met in the library meeting room for forty minutes after the November table.","Three things were decided. First, the table will add a third trestle in January, for bicycles, because brakes and chains were turned away four times in the autumn for want of space. Second, the one-item rule stays, but the one o'clock exception will now be printed on the notice instead of being explained to each person in the line. Third, the table will not take bookings.","The booking question was the only one that split the room. Three volunteers argued for a booking sheet, saying it would cut the waiting and let more items through in four hours. The rest disagreed, on the grounds that the waiting is where people talk to each other. The vote was eight to three.","One item was carried over. Nobody has agreed to copy the tally sheet into a spreadsheet, and the paper sheet is now two inches thick."].join("\n\n");const WORDS=["irreparable","reliability","demonstration","conscientious","volunteering","mechanism","insulation","apprentice","perseverance","incompatible","unmistakable","generosity"];const WRITE_DIR="Write on your paper for about 5 minutes. Then hold it up or read it to your teacher.";const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const pills=(...w)=>`<div class="row center" style="margin-top:18px;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap">${w.map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.2rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`;const lookBack=(label,inner)=>`<details class="look-back" open><summary>Look back at ${label}</summary>${inner}</details>`;const group=(inner,label)=>`<div style="text-align:center">${inner}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:4px 0 0">${label}</p></div>`;const lead=t=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">${t}</p>`;const paras=t=>t.split(/\n\s*\n/).map(p=>`<p>${p}</p>`).join("");const srcLine=t=>`<p class="src" style="margin:0 0 6px;font-size:1rem">${t}</p>`;const artHtml=small=>F.passage(ART,ART_TITLE,!!small);const threeTexts=()=>`<style>body:not(.sup-big) .texts article p{font-size:1.04rem;line-height:1.5}</style><div class="texts"><article>${srcLine(ART_SRC)}<div class="passage sm">${paras(ART)}</div></article><article>${srcLine(NOTICE_SRC)}<div class="passage sm">${paras(NOTICE)}</div></article><article>${srcLine(LETTER_SRC)}<div class="passage sm">${paras(LETTER)}</div></article></div>`;const factBox=(title,items)=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;background:var(--surface);padding:10px 20px;margin:14px 0"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0 0 6px;text-align:left">${title}</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.2em;font-size:1.22rem;line-height:1.5">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul></div>`;function noteBoxes(a,b,c){const bx=t=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;min-width:220px;min-height:96px;padding:8px 14px;background:var(--surface)"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0;font-size:1.22rem">${t}</p></div>`;return`<div class="figure" style="flex-wrap:wrap">${bx(a)}${bx(b)}${bx(c)}</div>`}const tallyTable=()=>F.table(["What came to the table","Items in 26 months"],[["Small electrics","214"],["Textiles","177"],["Everything else","220"],["Total","611"]],{caption:"The tally sheet, 26 months"});function tileFig(){const n=10,u=34,ox=72,oy=40,W=ox+n*u+26,H=oy+n*u+52;const T=(x,y,t,a)=>`<text x="${x}" y="${y}" text-anchor="${a||"middle"}" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">${t}</text>`;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W} ${H}" width="${W}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="A one meter square cut into a 10 by 10 grid of small squares, each one tenth of a meter on a side. A heavy outlined block in the top left corner is 8 squares across and 5 squares down, and those 40 squares are shaded.">`;for(let r=0;r<n;r++)for(let c=0;c<n;c++){const on=r<5&&c<8;s+=`<rect x="${ox+c*u}" y="${oy+r*u}" width="${u}" height="${u}" fill="${on?"var(--gold)":"var(--surface)"}" fill-opacity="${on?".55":"1"}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="1.6"/>`}s+=`<rect x="${ox}" y="${oy}" width="${8*u}" height="${5*u}" fill="none" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="4.5"/>`;s+=T(ox+4*u,oy-13,"8 tiles across")+T(ox-12,oy+2.5*u,"5 tiles","end")+T(ox-12,oy+2.5*u+20,"down","end")+T(ox+n*u/2,oy+n*u+26,"The whole square is 1 meter by 1 meter.")+T(ox+n*u/2,oy+n*u+46,"Each small tile is 1/10 of a meter on a side.");return s+"</svg>"}const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Tally Sheet Words",short:"Long Words",parts:[{std:["RF.5.3"],kind:"words",words:WORDS,n:12,dur:80,dir:"Read each word out loud. Break it into chunks you know, then put it back together.",desc:"reading unfamiliar multisyllabic words using letter sounds, syllable patterns, and word parts",key:["Read each word in order: irreparable, reliability, demonstration, conscientious, volunteering, mechanism, insulation, apprentice, perseverance, incompatible, unmistakable, generosity.","Watch for prefixes that mean not: ir- in irreparable, in- in incompatible, un- in unmistakable. Watch for suffixes: -able, -ity, -tion, -ous, -ing, -ism, -ice, -ance.","Watch for chunking rather than guessing from the first syllable: ir-rep-a-ra-ble, re-li-a-bil-i-ty, per-se-ver-ance, gen-er-os-i-ty. conscientious is the hardest: con-sci-en-tious, where sci says sh.","Optional meaning check: what does the ir- do in irreparable (cannot be repaired), and what root do you hear inside reliability (rely)?"]}]},{id:2,title:"What Comes to the Table",short:"The Tally",parts:[{std:["RI.5.1"],dur:150,dir:"Read the article. Then answer out loud. Quote the article word for word to prove each answer.",desc:"quoting accurately from an informational text to explain it and to draw inferences",html:()=>artHtml(true),qs:["How many items have come to the table in all, and over how long? Quote the words.","How many of those items have gone home working? Quote the sentence and say how the writer gets to that number.","Quote the sentence that tells you the table does not take money.","The article never says the volunteers care more about the people than about the things. Quote the sentence that most strongly supports that inference, and explain how it supports it.","Quote accurately to support this inference: most of a visit to the repair table is not spent being helped."],key:["611 items over 26 months. Quotes “Over those 26 months, 611 items have come to the table.” (RI.5.1, quoting to explain)","432. Quotes “44 of those came back the next month with the part and were finished then, so 432 items in all have gone home working.” She adds the 388 that left working the same day to the 44 that came back with a part. (RI.5.1, quoting to explain)","Quotes “The table charges nothing and accepts nothing,” or “There is a jar on the end of the table, and it is for the library.” (RI.5.1, quoting to explain)","Quotes “What the tally sheet does not record is the part the volunteers talk about most, which is that people stay.” It supports the inference because the writer says the thing the volunteers talk about most is not on the tally sheet at all, so the counting of items is not what they value most. (RI.5.1, quoting to support an inference)","Quotes “The average visit, timed over one Saturday in November, was 52 minutes, and most of that was waiting.” The word most does the work: more than half of the 52 minutes was waiting rather than repair. (RI.5.1, quoting to support an inference)"]}]},{id:3,title:"Inside the Brackets",short:"Brackets",parts:[{std:["5.OA.A.1"],dur:160,dir:"Work each one on your paper and hold it up. Say which grouping symbol you worked first.",desc:"using parentheses, brackets, and braces, and evaluating expressions that contain them",html:()=>lead("The volunteers work out the month's totals on the back of the tally sheet. Work the innermost grouping symbol first."),qs:["Evaluate 6 × (14 &minus; 9)","Evaluate 2 × [8 + (15 ÷ 3)]","Evaluate {4 × (7 + 2)} ÷ 6","Put parentheses into 3 + 5 × 4 so that the value is 32.","Which is greater, 24 &minus; (6 + 3) or (24 &minus; 6) + 3? By how much?","Evaluate 50 &minus; [3 × (12 &minus; 8)]","Evaluate 18 ÷ (2 + 1) + 7"],key:["30. Work inside the parentheses first: 14 − 9 = 5, then 6 × 5 = 30. Partial: 75 comes from multiplying before subtracting and does not count. (5.OA.A.1)","26. Innermost first: 15 ÷ 3 = 5. Then the brackets: 8 + 5 = 13. Then 2 × 13 = 26. Partial: 13 found by stopping inside the brackets is approaching. (5.OA.A.1)","6. Innermost first: 7 + 2 = 9. Then the braces: 4 × 9 = 36. Then 36 ÷ 6 = 6. Partial: 36 found by stopping before the division is approaching. (5.OA.A.1)","(3 + 5) × 4 = 32, because 3 + 5 = 8 and 8 × 4 = 32. Without the parentheses the value is 23, because multiplying comes first. Partial: parentheses placed anywhere that does not give 32 does not count. (5.OA.A.1)","(24 − 6) + 3 is greater, by 6. The first is 24 − 9 = 15. The second is 18 + 3 = 21, and 21 − 15 = 6. Partial: the greater one named with no difference given is approaching. (5.OA.A.1)","38. Innermost first: 12 − 8 = 4. Then the brackets: 3 × 4 = 12. Then 50 − 12 = 38. Partial: 12 found by stopping inside the brackets is approaching. (5.OA.A.1)","13. Parentheses first: 2 + 1 = 3. Then 18 ÷ 3 = 6. Then 6 + 7 = 13. The + 7 is outside the division, so it is not part of it. Partial: taking the + 7 inside the division does not count. (5.OA.A.1)"]}]},{id:4,title:"Read the Setting Up",short:"Read Aloud",parts:[{std:["RF.5.4"],kind:"read",n:0,label:"words read correctly",dur:130,dir:"Read the passage out loud. Use your best reading voice.",desc:"a grade-level passage read aloud",html:()=>F.passage(FLU,FLU_TITLE),key:["__WC__ words, not counting the title. Time 1 minute, mark the last word read, and count errors and self-corrections on your own copy. Let the student finish the passage.","Winter grade 5 benchmark (Hasbrouck and Tindal 2017): 50th percentile 133 words correct per minute, 25th percentile 109, 10th percentile 84.","Note pace, phrasing, and expression. Listen for the pauses at the commas in the long first sentence, and for the list in the third paragraph. Self-corrections are a good sign that the student is reading for meaning."]}]},{id:5,title:"The Second Table",short:"The Story",parts:[{std:["RL.5.1"],dur:150,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud. Quote the story word for word to prove each answer.",desc:"quoting accurately from a story to explain it and to draw inferences",html:()=>F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true),qs:["Why did Ines sign up for the repair table in October? Quote the words.","How long did the bag strap take, and who did the sewing? Quote the words.","Quote accurately to support this inference: Ines respects Mr. Wendt even though she did not want his table.","The narrator says the apologizing stopping “was the actual repair.” What does that mean? Quote the words around it that support your answer.","At the end Ines writes her name in the textiles column. Quote one line from the beginning of the story and one from the end that together show this is a change."],key:["Quotes “mostly to get out of a Saturday chess club she had stopped liking in September.” (RL.5.1, quoting to explain)","Forty minutes, and the seven-year-old girl did the sewing. Quotes “It took forty minutes” and “The girl said she wanted to.” (RL.5.1, quoting to explain)","Quotes “does the zipper work with a seam ripper and a pair of pliers and a steadiness Ines could not have faked.” The phrase “could not have faked” says she judges the skill as real and beyond her, which is respect. (RL.5.1, quoting to support an inference)","It means the thing that got mended was the girl's readiness to make a mistake without saying sorry for it, which matters more to Ines than the strap. Quotes “the girl kept pulling the thread back out by accident and then apologizing, and by about the fifth time the apologizing stopped.” (RL.5.1, quoting to support an inference)","Beginning: “which Ines had been assigned to without being asked,” or “You will get electrics in March.” End: “Then she wrote her name in the textiles column, which was shorter, and slid it back.” At the start the textiles table is something done to her and electrics is the prize. At the end she chooses textiles over the table she was waiting for. (RL.5.1, quoting to support an inference)"]}]},{id:6,title:"Thread and Cloth",short:"Fraction ×",parts:[{std:["5.NF.B.4"],dur:180,dir:"Use the tiled square for numbers 3, 4 and 5. Work on your paper and hold it up. Say the unit in every answer.",desc:"multiplying a whole number or a fraction by a fraction, and area with fractional side lengths",html:()=>lead("The textiles table measures cloth and thread in fractions of a meter."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${group(tileFig(),"One square meter of cloth, tiled")}</div>`,qs:["Work out 2/3 × 12. Then say in words what 2/3 of 12 means, using the idea of equal parts.","Mr. Wendt uses 2/3 of a 9 meter spool of thread. How many meters does he use?","Look at the tiled square. The heavy rectangle is 8 tiles across and 5 tiles down. What are its two side lengths in meters? Give each one in its simplest form.","The same heavy rectangle. How many small tiles does it cover, and what fraction of a square meter is one tile worth?","So what is the area of the heavy rectangle in square meters? Then multiply the two side lengths and say whether the two answers match.","A patch of cloth is 3/4 of a meter long and 2/3 of a meter wide. What is its area?"],key:["8. Split 12 into 3 equal parts of 4, then take 2 of them: 2 × 4 = 8. In words, 2/3 of 12 is 2 of the 3 equal parts that 12 is split into. Or: 2/3 × 12 = 24/3 = 8. Partial: 8 found with no words about equal parts is approaching. (5.NF.B.4a)","6 meters. Split 9 into 3 equal parts of 3, then take 2 of them: 2 × 3 = 6. As an equation, 2/3 × 9 = 18/3 = 6. Partial: 3 is one part and not two, so it is approaching. (5.NF.B.4a)","8/10 of a meter, which is 4/5 of a meter, by 5/10 of a meter, which is 1/2 of a meter. Each tile is 1/10 of a meter on a side, so 8 tiles across is 8 tenths and 5 tiles down is 5 tenths. Partial: 8/10 by 5/10 right with neither one put in simplest form is approaching. (5.NF.B.4b, tiling a rectangle with unit fraction squares)","40 tiles, and one tile is worth 1/100 of a square meter. The count is 8 × 5 = 40. Each tile is 1/10 by 1/10, and a hundred of them fill the square meter, so each is 1/100. Partial: 40 right with the tile called 1/10 of a square meter instead of 1/100 is approaching. (5.NF.B.4b, tiling a rectangle with unit fraction squares)","40/100 of a square meter, which is 2/5. Multiplying the sides gives 4/5 × 1/2 = 4/10 = 2/5, so the two ways match. Partial: 40/100 right with the multiplication not carried out, or carried out but not compared, is approaching. (5.NF.B.4b, tiling a rectangle with unit fraction squares)","1/2 of a square meter. 3/4 × 2/3 = 6/12, and 6/12 reduces to 1/2. Partial: 6/12 right with no simpler form given is approaching. (5.NF.B.4b)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Three Accounts",short:"3 Texts",parts:[{std:["RI.5.9"],dur:165,dir:"Read all three texts. Then answer out loud. Each answer has to use more than one of the texts.",desc:"integrating information from several texts on the same topic in order to speak about it knowledgeably",html:threeTexts,qs:["Using all three texts, explain what the repair table is and what it is not. Say which text each part of your answer comes from.","Two of the texts mention the 52 minute average visit. What does each one do with that number?","Which rule on the notice does the letter say matters most? Use the article and the letter together to explain why the volunteers wrote that rule.","Put one fact from each of the three texts together to answer this: why does the table let children work on their own item?","A reader says, “The table is a free repair shop.” Use at least two of the texts to answer that."],key:["It is a volunteer table that runs one Saturday a month for four hours under the library awning (Text 1 and Text 2), where people bring one item, stay with it, and may be told it cannot be fixed (Text 2). It is not a repair shop, not a paid service, and not a drop-off: Text 2 says the table does not take money and will not work on anything left behind, and Text 3 says outright that “a drop-off table is a free repair shop, which is a fine thing, but it is not what we are.” Full credit needs parts drawn from at least two texts, with the texts named. (RI.5.9, integrating information from several texts)","Text 1 reports it as a measurement: “The average visit, timed over one Saturday in November, was 52 minutes, and most of that was waiting.” Text 3 takes the same number and argues it is not a problem: “Fifty-two minutes is how long it takes two neighbors to find out they live on the same street.” The number is the same in both. What it means is not. (RI.5.9, same fact used two ways)","Rule 2, stay with your item. The letter says they wrote it because the first six months were people dropping bags off, and that the reason to stay is that you watch and then can do it yourself next time. The article backs this up from the other side: what the tally sheet does not record is “that people stay,” and the average visit runs 52 minutes. (RI.5.9, two texts on one point)","Any answer that uses all three. Text 2 rule 5 says children may work on their own item with a volunteer beside them. Text 3 gives the reason: people who watch a repair twice can do the third one themselves, and a seven-year-old sewed a strap over forty minutes while neither volunteer touched the bag. Text 1 shows why that matters to the table: the thing the volunteers talk about most is that people stay, and it is not on the tally sheet. (RI.5.9, integrating three texts)","The reader is wrong, and two texts say so. Text 2 rule 4: “The repair table does not take money,” but rule 2 and rule 3 also mean it is not a shop, because you have to stay and nothing is guaranteed. Text 3 names the difference directly: a drop-off table would be a free repair shop, and that is not what the table is. Accept a partial agreement if it is supported, for example that the table does mend things for nothing (Text 1), but the student has to use the texts to say where the comparison breaks down. (RI.5.9, using several texts to answer a claim)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Fix the Minutes",short:"Fix It",parts:[{title:"One Mistake Each",std:["L.5.1"],dur:90,dir:"Each line from the volunteers' minutes has one mistake. Say the whole line out loud the right way.",desc:"fixing spoken grammar mistakes (verb tense, tense shifts, correlative conjunctions, prepositions)",qs:["By November the table had ran for 26 months.","Ines carried the trestles out and then sets up the thread box.","Either the brakes or the chain need a new cable.","Three volunteers argued for a booking sheet, but the rest of them was against it.","The folding chair is for whoever is next in the line, and it sits between of the two tables."],key:["By November the table had run for 26 months. The past perfect is had plus the past participle, which is run, not ran. (L.5.1b, perfect verb tense)","Ines carried the trestles out and then set up the thread box. Both verbs belong in the past. (L.5.1d, correcting an inappropriate shift in verb tense)","Either the brakes or the chain needs a new cable. With either or, the verb matches the nearer subject, which is chain. (L.5.1a, correlative conjunctions)","Three volunteers argued for a booking sheet, but the rest of them were against it. (L.5.1, subject-verb agreement inside a compound sentence)","The folding chair is for whoever is next in the line, and it sits between the two tables. between is the preposition on its own, with no of. (L.5.1a, prepositions)"]},{title:"Say It Right",std:["L.5.1"],dur:70,dir:"Answer out loud. Say whole sentences.",desc:"spoken grammar items (perfect tenses, correlative conjunctions, interjections, conjunctions)",qs:["Change this so it tells about something still going on: Mr. Wendt sat at the textiles table for 26 months. Start with <b>Mr. Wendt has</b> ____.","Change this so it tells about something that will be finished later: By March, Ines works nine Saturdays. Start with <b>By March, Ines will</b> ____.","Fill in the pair: ____ the brakes ____ the chain can be fixed at the table today. Use a pair that means both of them can.","Name the interjection in this sentence, and say what job it is doing: “Well, that clarinet is beyond us.”"],key:["Mr. Wendt has sat at the textiles table for 26 months. The present perfect, has plus the past participle, shows the sitting started in the past and still goes on. (L.5.1b, present perfect)","By March, Ines will have worked nine Saturdays. The future perfect is will have plus the past participle. (L.5.1b, future perfect)","Both the brakes and the chain can be fixed at the table today. both and is the pair that means the two together. Not only but also is also acceptable. (L.5.1a, correlative conjunctions)","Well. It is an interjection. It does not join anything or describe anything. It marks a pause and softens the bad news that comes after it. (L.5.1a, interjections)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Listen to the Minutes",short:"Listen",parts:[{std:["SL.5.2"],n:5,dur:140,dir:"Listen while your teacher reads the minutes. Then answer out loud. For the last two, use the table.",desc:"summarizing a text read aloud and information presented in a table",read:MINUTES_READ,html:()=>lead("Your teacher will read you the minutes of the volunteers' November meeting. Listen for what was decided and what was not. Then look at the tally table."),fig:()=>F.row([tallyTable(),"What the tally sheet records"]),qs:["Summarize the minutes in three or four sentences.","What were the three decisions?","Which decision split the room, what were the two sides, and how did the vote go?","Summarize what the table shows, in one sentence.","Put the minutes and the table together. Does the table support the decision to add a trestle for bicycles? Explain what the table can and cannot tell you about that."],key:["A summary of the whole, not a retelling, for example: Eleven volunteers met for forty minutes after the November table and made three decisions. They will add a third trestle for bicycles in January, they will keep the one-item rule but print the one o'clock exception on the notice, and they will not take bookings. The booking vote was eight to three. Nobody agreed to copy the tally sheet into a spreadsheet. (SL.5.2, summarizing a text read aloud)","A third trestle for bicycles in January; the one-item rule stays, with the one o'clock exception printed on the notice; and no bookings. (SL.5.2, supporting details)","The booking question. Three volunteers wanted a booking sheet because it would cut the waiting and let more items through in four hours. The rest wanted no bookings because the waiting is where people talk to each other. The vote was eight to three against bookings. (SL.5.2, details from a text read aloud)","A one sentence summary, for example: Over 26 months, 611 items came to the table, and small electrics and textiles together account for less than two thirds of them. Accept any accurate one sentence summary that names the total and the pattern. (SL.5.2, summarizing information in a table)","Partly. The table shows that Everything else, at 220 items, is the biggest of the three rows, bigger than textiles at 177, so there is clearly demand outside the two existing tables. But the table does not break Everything else apart, so it cannot show how many of those 220 were bicycles. The reason for the trestle comes from the minutes, not the table: brakes and chains were turned away four times in the autumn. (SL.5.2, integrating a read-aloud text with a table)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Tell Them What It Is",short:"Report",parts:[{std:["SL.5.4"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:130,dir:"Plan for 1 minute. Then talk for 1 to 2 minutes. Look at the camera and speak clearly.",desc:"a 1 to 2 minute spoken report with facts and descriptive details, in a logical order",html:()=>`<p class="big">Report on the Westmill repair table to somebody who has never been. Say what it is, how it works, and what you would see if you went. Use at least three facts, and at least one number from the table.</p>${F.row([tallyTable(),"Use a number from here"])}${pills("What it is","How it works","Facts and numbers","What you would see")}`,key:["Sequences the ideas logically: what the table is, then how it works, then what a visit looks like (SL.5.4)","Uses at least three appropriate facts, such as the second Saturday of the month, ten until two, one item per person, stay with your item, nothing is guaranteed, no money (SL.5.4)","Uses at least one number from the table accurately, such as 611 items in 26 months, 214 small electrics, 177 textiles, or 220 in everything else (SL.5.4)","Adds relevant descriptive details that help the listener picture it, such as the awning, the two trestles, the line, the folding chair for whoever is next (SL.5.4)","Speaks clearly at an understandable pace, with enough volume, for 1 to 2 minutes (SL.5.4)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Three Sources",short:"Notes",parts:[{std:["W.5.8"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:145,dir:"Read all three texts. Take your notes on your paper and hold them up.",desc:"summarizing or paraphrasing information from several sources in notes, and listing the sources",html:()=>`${threeTexts()}<p class="big">Make three boxes on your paper and label them like the boxes below. Put at least two notes in each box, and mark every note with the text it came from. Under the boxes, write a list of your three sources.</p>${noteBoxes("How the table runs","What the numbers say","Why the volunteers do it this way")}<p class="small" style="text-align:center;font-size:1.2rem">Put each note in your own words. A note that is copied word for word only counts if you put quotation marks around it.</p>`,key:["How it runs box: at least two notes, such as second Saturday of the month, ten until two, under the library awning, one item each, stay with your item, nothing is guaranteed, no money, children may work on their own item. (W.5.8, summarizing relevant information)","Numbers box: at least two notes, such as 26 months, 611 items in all, 432 went home working, 214 small electrics, 96 of them lamps, 177 textiles, 220 everything else, average visit 52 minutes. (W.5.8, summarizing relevant information)","Why box: at least two notes, such as a drop-off table would be a repair shop and that is not what they are, people who watch twice can do it themselves, the waiting is when neighbors talk, what the volunteers talk about most is that people stay. (W.5.8, paraphrasing)","Notes are in the student's own words unless quoted, every note is marked with its text, and a list of all three sources appears at the bottom with the title and the author or publisher of each. (W.5.8, paraphrasing and providing a list of sources)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Awning Desk",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"opinion",label:"Opinion",icon:"💬",hint:"Should the table take bookings?"},std:["W.5.1"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:215,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an opinion writing piece built from a fact box",html:()=>`<p class="big">The volunteers voted in November not to take bookings, and the question is coming back in the spring. Should the repair table take bookings? Write your opinion. Use the fact box. Write 8 to 10 sentences.</p>${factBox("Fact box: the booking question",["The table runs one Saturday a month, ten until two. That is four hours.","The average visit, timed on one Saturday in November, was 52 minutes, and most of that was waiting.","Eleven volunteers voted in November. Three wanted a booking sheet, eight did not. ","The three said a booking sheet would cut the waiting and let more items through in four hours.","The eight said the waiting is where people talk to each other.","Over 26 months the table has taken 611 items. 121 of them needed a part the table did not have, and 44 of those came back the next month and were finished.","Rule 2 on the library notice says stay with your item. The volunteers wrote it after six months of people dropping bags off."])}${checklist(["Introduce the topic and state your opinion clearly","Give reasons supported by facts from the fact box, in an order that makes sense","Link your opinion and your reasons with words and phrases: because, consequently, specifically, on the other hand","Name the strongest thing the other side would say, and answer it","End with a conclusion that follows from your reasons"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly and states an opinion, then organizes the reasons in an order that makes sense (W.5.1a)","Supports the opinion with logically ordered reasons backed by facts and details from the fact box (W.5.1b)","Uses the numbers accurately rather than inventing them, for example four hours, 52 minutes, eight to three, 611 items (W.5.1b)","Links opinion and reasons with words, phrases, and clauses such as because, consequently, specifically, on the other hand (W.5.1c)","Names the strongest point on the other side and answers it, and ends with a concluding statement that follows from the argument (W.5.1a and W.5.1d)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"facts",label:"Explain",icon:"📘",hint:"Teach someone how the table works"},std:["W.5.2"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:215,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an informative writing piece built from several sources",html:()=>`<p class="big">A town twenty miles away wants to start a repair table and has asked how Westmill's works. Write an explanation for them. Use facts from the three texts. Write 8 to 10 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Introduce the topic clearly and group related information together","Develop it with facts, definitions, concrete details, and quotations from the texts","Link ideas within and across paragraphs: in addition, for example, as a result, however","Use words that belong to the topic, such as volunteer, tally sheet, trestle, guarantee","End with a section or sentence that wraps it up"])}${lookBack("the three texts",threeTexts())}`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly and groups related information together (W.5.2a)","Develops the topic with facts, concrete details, and at least one quotation or specific number from the texts, such as one Saturday a month ten until two, one item each, 611 items in 26 months (W.5.2b)","Explains the rules and the reasons behind them, in particular stay with your item (W.5.2b)","Links ideas with words, phrases, and clauses such as in addition, for example, as a result, however (W.5.2c)","Uses words that belong to the topic and ends with a concluding statement or section (W.5.2d and W.5.2e)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"story",label:"Story",icon:"✨",hint:"An hour at a table of strangers"},std:["W.5.3"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:215,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a narrative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">You carry something broken to a table of people you do not know, and the rule is that you have to stay with it. Write the story of the hour you spend there. Write 9 to 11 sentences.</p>${pills("At first","A few minutes later","By the time","In the end")}${checklist(["Set up the situation and introduce the narrator and the other people","Tell the events in an order that unfolds naturally","Use dialogue, description, and pacing to show what happened","Use transitions that manage the order of events: at first, after a while, by the time, in the end","Use precise words and sensory details, and end with a conclusion that reflects on the hour"])}`,key:["Orients the reader by setting up the situation and introducing a narrator and other characters (W.5.3a)","Events unfold naturally in an order the reader can follow (W.5.3a)","Uses narrative techniques such as dialogue, description, and pacing to develop the events and the people (W.5.3b)","Uses a variety of transitional words, phrases, and clauses to manage the sequence (W.5.3c)","Uses concrete words, phrases, and sensory details, and ends with a conclusion that follows from and reflects on the events (W.5.3d and W.5.3e)"]}]},{id:13,title:"What the Words Mean",short:"Word Meaning",parts:[{std:["RL.5.4"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Look back at the story. Then answer out loud.",desc:"working out the meaning of words and phrases in a story, including figurative language",html:()=>lookBack("the story",F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true)),qs:["The story says Mr. Wendt was three jobs deep. What does that phrase mean?","Ines thinks the apologizing stopping was the actual repair. What does she mean by that?","The girl came under the awning with a face that said she had been told to wait her turn. A face cannot say anything. What is that phrase doing?"],key:["He already had three repairs waiting or in hand, so he was too far into the queue to take another. The word deep is being used for how far into a pile of work he was, not for distance down","The bag strap was only part of it. What really got mended was the girl losing the need to say sorry, that is, her confidence. Any answer naming the change in the girl rather than the bag counts","It is figurative. The writer gives the face the power to speak, so that the girl's expression tells you what she had been told and how she felt about it. Naming it as figurative language, or as the writer describing a look, both count"]}]},{id:14,title:"Ten Times and a Tenth",short:"Places",parts:[{std:["5.NBT.A.1"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"knowing that a digit is worth ten times the place to its right and one tenth of the place to its left",qs:["In the number 222, what is each 2 worth? How does each one compare with the 2 on its right?","In 4.44, the 4 in the hundredths place is what fraction of the 4 in the tenths place?","0.7 is ten times what number?"],key:["200, 20, and 2. Each 2 is worth ten times the 2 on its right, and one tenth of the 2 on its left. Partial: the three values right with no comparison is approaching.","One tenth. 0.04 is one tenth of 0.4. Partial: 10 times as much reverses the comparison and does not count.","0.07. Partial: 7 goes the wrong way along the places and does not count."]}]},{id:15,title:"Decimals on the Sheet",short:"Decimals",parts:[{std:["5.NBT.A.3"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Write the first answer on your paper. Say the other two out loud.",desc:"reading, writing, and comparing decimals to thousandths",qs:["Write 3.408 in expanded form, then say it in words.","Compare 0.52 and 0.509. Say it with a symbol.","Put these in order from least to greatest: 1.7, 1.07, 1.77"],key:["3 + 0.4 + 0.008, or 3 + (4 x 1/10) + (8 x 1/1000). In words: three and four hundred eight thousandths. Partial: the expanded form right with no words, or the reverse, is approaching.","0.52 > 0.509. Both have 5 tenths, then 2 hundredths beats 0 hundredths. A student who says 0.509 is bigger because it has more digits has the common error. Or: compare 520 thousandths against 509 thousandths.","1.07, 1.7, 1.77. Or: line the three up as hundredths, 1.07, 1.70, 1.77. Partial: two of the three in order is approaching."]}]},{id:16,title:"Unlike Bottoms",short:"Add Fractions",parts:[{title:"Add and Subtract",std:["5.NF.A.1"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Solve on your paper. Say each answer.",desc:"adding and subtracting fractions and mixed numbers with unlike denominators",qs:["3/4 + 2/5","5/6 - 1/4","2 1/3 + 1 1/2"],key:["23/20, or 1 and 3/20. Twentieths is the common denominator: 15/20 + 8/20. Or: fortieths work too, 30/40 + 16/40 = 46/40. Partial: 5/9 comes from adding across and does not count.","7/12. Twelfths is the common denominator: 10/12 - 3/12. Partial: subtracting across, numerator from numerator and denominator from denominator, does not count.","3 and 5/6. Sixths: 2 and 2/6 plus 1 and 3/6. Or: change both to improper fractions, 14/6 + 9/6 = 23/6. Partial: 3 2/5 comes from adding across and does not count."]},{title:"Does That Make Sense",std:["5.NF.A.2"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Solve on your paper. Say your answer and how you checked it.",desc:"fraction word problems with unlike denominators, checked against benchmark fractions",qs:["A volunteer used 2/3 of a reel of thread in the morning and 1/4 of it in the afternoon. How much of the reel did they use? Is that more or less than one whole reel?","A bag strap needs 5/6 of a meter of webbing. There is 1/2 a meter left in the box. How much more is needed?","Somebody says 1/2 + 1/3 = 2/5. Use a benchmark to show that cannot be right."],key:["11/12 of the reel (8/12 + 3/12), which is less than one whole reel. Partial: 11/12 right with nothing said about one whole reel is approaching.","1/3 of a meter (5/6 - 3/6 = 2/6). Partial: 2/6 right with no simpler form given is approaching.","Adding something to 1/2 has to give more than 1/2, and 2/5 is less than 1/2. Look for the benchmark, not for the correct answer of 5/6. Partial: giving 5/6 with no benchmark used is approaching."]}]},{id:17,title:"Share the Reels",short:"Share",parts:[{std:["5.NF.B.3"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Answer out loud. Write the last answer on your paper.",desc:"reading a fraction as division and solving sharing problems with fraction answers",qs:["3 reels of thread are shared equally among 4 volunteers. How much does each one get? Write it as a division and as a fraction.","What division does the fraction 7/8 stand for?","17 meters of webbing is cut into 5 equal pieces. How long is each piece?"],key:["3 divided by 4, which is 3/4 of a reel each. Partial: one of the division and the fraction given is approaching.","7 divided by 8. Partial: 8 divided by 7 reverses the division and does not count.","17/5 meters, which is 3 and 2/5 meters. A remainder answer such as 3 remainder 2 misses the point of this standard. Partial: 3 remainder 2 is approaching, since the leftover has to be shared too."]}]},{id:18,title:"Boxes Under the Table",short:"Volume",parts:[{title:"Count the Cubes",std:["5.MD.C.3"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Look at the stack of parts boxes. Answer out loud.",desc:"understanding volume as the number of unit cubes that fill a solid",fig:()=>F.row(F.prism(4,3,2,{unit:34})),qs:["A unit cube is one cube with every edge 1 unit long. What is its volume?","This solid is 4 cubes across, 3 cubes deep, and 2 cubes high. How many unit cubes fill it? What is its volume?","Why is volume measured in cubic units and not in square units?"],key:["1 cubic unit. Partial: 1 with no unit named is approaching.","24 unit cubes, so the volume is 24 cubic units. Or: count one layer of 12 and double it. Partial: the count right with no volume named is approaching.","Because volume fills a space that has length, width, and height, so the unit has to be a cube. Square units only cover a flat surface. Partial: naming the three directions with nothing said about square units covering a flat surface is approaching."]},{title:"Length Times Width Times Height",std:["5.MD.C.5"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Solve on your paper. Say your answer with its unit.",desc:"using the volume formula for rectangular prisms, including a missing side and two stacked boxes",qs:["A parts box is 12 cm long, 8 cm wide, and 5 cm high. What is its volume?","Another box holds 240 cubic cm. Its base is 8 cm by 6 cm. How tall is it?","Two boxes sit one on the other. One is 4 by 3 by 2 and the other is 4 by 3 by 3. What is the total volume?"],key:["480 cubic cm (12 x 8 x 5). The unit has to be cubic. Or: 12 × 8 = 96 for the base, then 96 × 5.","5 cm (240 divided by 48). Or: 240 ÷ 8 = 30, then 30 ÷ 6 = 5.","60 cubic units. 24 + 36, or 4 x 3 x 5, because the two stack into one 4 by 3 by 5 box. Partial: one of the two boxes worked out is approaching."]}]},{id:19,title:"Map the Awning",short:"Grid",parts:[{std:["5.G.A.1"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Look at the grid. Answer out loud.",desc:"reading and naming points on a coordinate grid in the first quadrant",html:()=>lead("The volunteers drew the awning on a grid. E is the electrics table, T is the textiles table, and C is the waiting chair."),fig:()=>F.row(F.coordGrid({min:0,max:10,size:380,points:[[2,7,"E"],[8,7,"T"],[5,3,"C"]]})),qs:["What are the coordinates of point E? Which number tells you how far across, and which tells you how far up?","Start at E. Move 3 to the right and 4 down. Which labeled point do you land on, and what are its coordinates?","What is the point (0, 0) called, and what do the two numbers of any point always measure from?"],key:["(2, 7). The first number, 2, is how far across from 0 along the horizontal axis. The second number, 7, is how far up the vertical axis. Partial: the pair right with only one number explained is approaching.","Point C, at (5, 3). From (2, 7), 3 right makes the first number 5 and 4 down makes the second number 3. Partial: the point named with no coordinates given is approaching.","The origin. Both numbers are measured from the origin, the first one along the horizontal axis and the second one along the vertical axis. Partial: naming the origin with nothing said about measuring from it is approaching."]}]},{id:20,title:"Squeezing Air",short:"Tiny Bits",parts:[{std:["5-PS1-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"using a model of particles too small to be seen to explain what happens to matter",html:()=>`<p class="big">A volunteer blocks the tip of a syringe with a finger and pushes the plunger. The air inside squashes into half the space. Then she fills the same syringe with water, blocks the tip, and pushes as hard as she can. The plunger will not move.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("drop",84)}${F.icon("hand",84)}${F.icon("wind",84)}</div>`,qs:["Air squashed into half the space. What does that tell you about the particles in air and the space between them?","The water would not squash at all. What does that tell you about the particles in a liquid?","Nothing left or entered the sealed syringe. Did the amount of air change when it was squashed? Explain."],key:["Air is mostly empty space with particles spread far apart, so the particles can be pushed closer together into a smaller volume. (5-PS1-1)","The particles in a liquid are already touching, with almost no gaps left to close, so there is nowhere for them to be pushed. (5-PS1-1)","No. The same number of particles was there the whole time, just packed into less space. The amount of matter did not change, only how much room it took up. (5-PS1-1)"]},{std:["5-PS1-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"identifying materials from measured properties rather than from appearance",html:()=>`<p class="big">A community sewing group tested four white fabrics, all of them cut to the same 20 cm square.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Fabric</th><th>Mass</th><th>Water soaked up</th><th>Stretches then springs back?</th><th>Held near a warm iron</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A</td><td>12 g</td><td>22 g</td><td>no</td><td>no change</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B</td><td>9 g</td><td>2 g</td><td>no</td><td>shrinks and goes shiny</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C</td><td>18 g</td><td>14 g</td><td>yes</td><td>no change</td></tr>
     <tr><td>D</td><td>26 g</td><td>31 g</td><td>no</td><td>no change</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The four fabrics are thin cotton, polyester, cotton jersey knit, and thick cotton towelling.</p>`,qs:["Which fabric is the polyester, and which result settles it?","The group needs cloth for a batch of wash cloths. Which fabric should they use, and which column decided it?","Fabric C is the only one that stretches and springs back. Which fabric is it, and why would stretch matter for a jumper?"],key:["B. It soaked up almost no water and it was the only one that shrank and went shiny near a warm iron, both of which point to a synthetic rather than cotton. (5-PS1-3)","D, the thick towelling. It soaked up 31 g, more than any other, and soaking up water is the whole job of a wash cloth. (5-PS1-3)","The cotton jersey knit. A knit stretches because of how the loops are made, so a jumper can pull over a head and move with a body instead of tearing. (5-PS1-3)"]}]},{id:21,title:"Nothing Lost",short:"Weigh In",parts:[{std:["5-PS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"using measurements to show that total weight is conserved through heating, cooling, and mixing",html:()=>`<p class="big">A baking group making loaves for a food bank weighed three sealed tubs before and after.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Sealed tub</th><th>What happened inside</th><th>Before</th><th>After</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>warm water, flour, and yeast, left four hours until the dough doubled in size</td><td>880 g</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>butter left in a warm spot until it melted completely</td><td>250 g</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>sugar stirred into warm water until nothing could be seen</td><td>530 g</td><td>530 g</td></tr></table>`,qs:["The dough in tub 1 doubled in size. What does the tub weigh afterwards? Explain.","Tub 2 went from solid butter to liquid butter. What does it weigh?","A baker says the dough must be lighter because it rose. Explain the mistake in that reasoning."],key:["880 g. The yeast made gas that filled the dough with bubbles, but the gas is matter and it is still in the sealed tub, so nothing was gained or lost. (5-PS1-2)","250 g. Melting rearranges the particles from solid to liquid without adding or removing any. (5-PS1-2)","Rising is a change in volume, not in weight. The dough takes up more room because gas bubbles are spread through it, and that gas was already inside the sealed tub. Bigger is not lighter. (5-PS1-2)"]},{std:["5-PS1-4"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"deciding from evidence whether mixing two substances made a new substance",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three mixings from the same baking session.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Mixing</th><th>What was seen</th></tr>
     <tr><td>baking soda and buttermilk</td><td>fizzed at once and the batter rose; afterwards neither the gritty soda nor the sour taste was there</td></tr>
     <tr><td>cornstarch and cold water</td><td>a thick cloudy liquid that felt firm when stirred fast and runny when left alone; left to stand, the cornstarch settled out</td></tr>
     <tr><td>egg white warmed in a pan</td><td>clear runny liquid turned solid and white, and cooling it down did not turn it clear again</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which two produced a new substance? Give the evidence for each.","Which one is a mixture, and what is the evidence?","The cornstarch mixture behaves oddly, going firm when stirred fast. Does odd behavior mean a new substance formed? Explain."],key:["The baking soda and buttermilk, because a gas fizzed off and neither starting substance was recognisable afterwards, and the egg white, because the change to solid white cannot be undone by cooling. (5-PS1-4)","The cornstarch and water. Left to stand, the cornstarch settled out unchanged, so it was only suspended in the water. (5-PS1-4)","No. Behaving strangely is not the test. The test is whether the starting materials are still there and can be got back, and here the cornstarch settles out unchanged, so it is still a mixture. (5-PS1-4)"]}]},{id:22,title:"A Plant With No Pot",short:"Plant Food",parts:[{std:["5-LS1-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing from measurements that plants get their material for growth from air and water",html:()=>`<p class="big">A class kept an air plant on a shelf for a year. It sat in a wire holder with no soil and no pot. Twice a week it was misted with plain water and nothing else was added.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("herb",80)}${F.icon("drop",80)}${F.icon("sun",80)}</div>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>At the start</th><th>After a year</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the plant, dried</td><td>6 g</td><td>21 g</td></tr>
     <tr><td>soil in the holder</td><td>none</td><td>none</td></tr>
     <tr><td>anything added</td><td colspan="2">plain water, misted, and daylight</td></tr></table>`,qs:["How much dry weight did the plant gain, and what were the only things ever given to it?","There was no soil at any point. Where did the new material come from?","A visitor says the plant must have been living on the water alone. Why is water not a complete answer?"],key:["It gained 15 g of dry weight. The only things given were plain water and daylight. (5-LS1-1)","From air and water. The plant takes carbon dioxide out of the air and water from the misting, and builds its own material from them using energy from daylight. (5-LS1-1)","Because the dry weight went up, and drying removes the water. The extra dry material has to have come from somewhere else, and that is mainly carbon taken from the air. (5-LS1-1)"]},{std:["5-PS3-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Look at the chain. Answer out loud.",desc:"using a model to trace the energy in an animal's food back to the sun",html:()=>`<p class="big">One path energy takes from a field to a meal handed to a neighbor.</p>
     <p class="huge" style="font-size:2.2rem;margin:10px 0;text-align:center">${F.icon("sun",46)} &rarr; ${F.icon("wheat",46)} &rarr; ${F.icon("bread",46)} &rarr; ${F.icon("hands",46)}</p>
     <p class="big">Wheat grown in summer is milled and baked, and the loaf is carried to a neighbor.</p>`,qs:["Trace the energy in that loaf back to its source.","Explain how the energy in the loaf gets used by the person who eats it. Name three things it is used for.","The same field could feed people directly, or feed animals that people then eat. Which path delivers more of the sun's energy to people, and why?"],key:["From the bread, which came from wheat, which captured energy from sunlight and stored it in the grain. (5-PS3-1)","It is used for body repair, for growth, for movement, and to keep the body warm. Any three of those. (5-PS3-1)","Feeding people directly. An animal uses up most of the energy in its food for its own moving, growing, and warmth, so only a small part is left in the meat or milk. Skipping that step keeps more of the sun's energy. (5-PS3-1)"]}]},{id:23,title:"The Worm Bin",short:"Matter Cycle",parts:[{std:["5-LS2-1"],n:4,dur:90,dir:"Look at the model. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing with a model how matter moves among plants, animals, decomposers, and the environment",html:()=>`<p class="big">A community garden feeds its kitchen scraps to a worm bin and spreads the result on the beds.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>How</th></tr>
     <tr><td>air and soil</td><td>garden vegetables</td><td>taken in through leaves and roots</td></tr>
     <tr><td>vegetables</td><td>the people at the garden</td><td>eaten</td></tr>
     <tr><td>peelings, stalks, and spoiled leaves</td><td>worms and the bacteria in the bin</td><td>eaten and broken down</td></tr>
     <tr><td>worm castings from the bin</td><td>the garden beds</td><td>spread as a soil improver</td></tr>
     <tr><td>garden beds</td><td>next year's vegetables</td><td>minerals taken up by roots</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("carrot",70)}${F.icon("hands",70)}${F.icon("snail",70)}${F.icon("sprout",70)}</div>`,qs:["Name the decomposers in this model and say what they do.","Follow one carbon atom from the air into a carrot, and then describe two different routes it could take afterwards.","The bin is fed only bread and nothing else for a month. It starts to smell and the worms crowd at the lid. What does that suggest a working bin needs?","A gardener buys bagged fertilizer instead of using the bin. Explain what the bin was doing that the bag does not."],key:["Worms and bacteria in the bin. They eat and break down dead plant material into simpler substances that plants can take up again. (5-LS2-1)","The carrot takes carbon dioxide from the air and builds the carbon into the root. After that it could be eaten by a person and breathed out again, or the peelings could go to the worm bin where decomposers release it as gas or leave it in the castings and then in the soil. (5-LS2-1)","A mix of materials and air moving through it. Decomposers need varied food, moisture that is not waterlogged, and air, and one material alone packs down and the bin goes sour. (5-LS2-1)","The bin returns the garden's own material to the garden, so minerals that left in the vegetables go back into the beds and the cycle closes. A bag brings minerals in from elsewhere and the peelings still have to go somewhere. (5-LS2-1)"]}]},{id:24,title:"Hanging Straight",short:"Down",parts:[{std:["5-PS2-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing that the gravitational force Earth exerts on objects is directed down",html:()=>`<p class="big">Paper hearts on strings are hung along the hall for a kindness week display. A dropped envelope slides under a chair.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("heart",80)}${F.icon("books",80)}${F.icon("hands",80)}</div>`,qs:["Every string hangs the same way whatever height it is tied at. Which way, and what makes them all line up?","One heart is taped flat to a sloping ceiling. It still hangs its string straight down rather than along the slope. Explain why.","A child says things fall because they are heavy, and light things do not fall. Name something light that still falls, and explain what really decides the direction."],key:["Straight down. Earth pulls each heart toward its center, which is straight down from wherever it hangs, so every string lines up with that one direction. (5-PS2-1)","The pull on the heart is from Earth, not from the ceiling. Whatever surface it is attached to, the pull is still straight down, so the string hangs down rather than following the slope. (5-PS2-1)","A paper heart, a feather, or a sheet of tissue all fall. Air slows a light wide thing down so it drifts, but the direction is the same for everything, because Earth's pull is directed down for heavy and light alike. (5-PS2-1)"]},{std:["5-ESS2-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the story. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing with an example how the geosphere, biosphere, hydrosphere, and atmosphere interact",html:()=>`<p class="big">The Earth system has four parts. The geosphere is rock and soil. The biosphere is living things. The hydrosphere is all the water. The atmosphere is air.</p>
     <p class="big">A neighborhood group plants forty trees along a bare street. Five years later the shade has dropped the summer pavement temperature by several degrees. The roots have broken up the hard-packed soil. Rain soaks in instead of racing to the drain. Birds and insects that were not there before are nesting in the branches.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("tree",72)}${F.icon("bluebird",72)}${F.icon("drop",72)}${F.icon("sun",72)}</div>`,qs:["Shade drops the pavement temperature. Which two spheres interact there?","Roots break up the hard soil so rain soaks in. Name the three spheres in that sentence and say what each does.","Birds and insects arrive that were not there before. Explain that as one part of the Earth system changing another."],key:["The biosphere and the atmosphere. Living trees block sunlight and cool the air and the surface beneath them. The geosphere is in it too, since the pavement is what cools. (5-ESS2-1)","The biosphere, the roots, changes the geosphere, the packed soil, which changes what the hydrosphere does, because rain now soaks in rather than running off. (5-ESS2-1)","A change in the biosphere, the new trees, created shelter, food, and nesting places, so other living things moved in. One part of the biosphere changed the conditions for another. (5-ESS2-1)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Near Light, Far Light",short:"Sky",parts:[{std:["5-ESS1-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Read both charts. Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing that the sun looks brightest because of how near it is, not because it gives off the most light",html:()=>`<p class="big">A class measured one flashlight with a light meter at different distances. The flashlight never changed.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Distance from the flashlight</th><th>Meter reading</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1 m</td><td>400</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2 m</td><td>100</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4 m</td><td>25</td></tr>
     <tr><td>8 m</td><td>6</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Now the same idea with stars.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Star</th><th>Light it gives off</th><th>Distance</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the sun</td><td>1 unit</td><td>8 light-minutes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Altair</td><td>about 10 units</td><td>17 light-years</td></tr></table>`,qs:["The flashlight bulb never changed. Describe what happened to the reading as the distance went up.","Use the flashlight readings to explain why Altair looks like a point of light while the sun lights up the whole sky.","A class member says the sun must be a huge, unusually powerful star. Use both charts to correct that."],key:["It dropped fast. Doubling the distance cut the reading to about a quarter each time: 400, then 100, then 25, then 6. (5-ESS1-1)","Light falls off very quickly with distance. Altair is 17 light-years away, unimaginably further than the sun at 8 light-minutes, so almost none of its light reaches us even though it gives off about ten times as much. (5-ESS1-1)","The sun is an ordinary star. Altair alone gives off about ten times more light, and it is only one of the nearer ones. The sun looks overwhelming only because it is by far the closest, exactly as the flashlight at 1 m outshone the same flashlight at 8 m. (5-ESS1-1)"]},{std:["5-ESS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"finding patterns in graphed data about shadows, day length, and the night sky",html:()=>`<p class="big">A class chalked round the shadow of the same bollard three times in one day, in February and again in June.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Time</th><th>February shadow</th><th>June shadow</th><th>Shadow points</th></tr>
     <tr><td>9 a.m.</td><td>190 cm</td><td>120 cm</td><td>west</td></tr>
     <tr><td>noon</td><td>95 cm</td><td>30 cm</td><td>north</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3 p.m.</td><td>205 cm</td><td>130 cm</td><td>east</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Describe the pattern within one day, and then the pattern between the two months.","The shadow points west in the morning and east in the afternoon. What is moving, and which way?","In June the class could see a bright group of stars overhead at 10 p.m. that was nowhere to be seen in February. What causes that?"],key:["Within a day the shadow is long in the morning, shortest at noon, and long again in the afternoon. Between the months, every June shadow is shorter than the February one at the same time of day. (5-ESS1-2)","Earth is spinning on its axis, which makes the sun appear to move across the sky from east to west, so the shadow swings the opposite way, from west to east. (5-ESS1-2)","Earth travels around the sun over the year, so at a given hour of the night we are facing a different part of space in June than in February and different stars are overhead. (5-ESS1-2)"]}]},{id:26,title:"Sharing the Water",short:"Water",parts:[{std:["5-ESS2-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the charts. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing the amounts and percentages of water and fresh water in Earth's reservoirs",html:()=>`<p class="big">All of Earth's water, and then the fresh part of it on its own.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>All water</th><th>Share</th></tr>
     <tr><td>salty ocean</td><td>about 97%</td></tr>
     <tr><td>fresh water</td><td>about 3%</td></tr></table>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>The fresh water</th><th>Share of the fresh part</th></tr>
     <tr><td>frozen in ice and glaciers</td><td>about 69%</td></tr>
     <tr><td>underground</td><td>about 30%</td></tr>
     <tr><td>lakes, rivers, soil, air, and living things</td><td>about 1%</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Out of all Earth's water, what share is fresh and not frozen? Show how you worked it out.","Living things are in the last row of the second chart. What does it mean to say some of Earth's fresh water is inside living things?","Describe the two graphs you would draw to tell this story. Then say why one graph on its own would not do it."],key:["About 1%. Fresh water is about 3% of all water, and about 31% of that is unfrozen, which is roughly 1% of the total. (5-ESS2-2)","Bodies are mostly water, so at any moment a large amount of the world's fresh water is inside plants, animals, and people, moving through them and back out. (5-ESS2-2)","A first graph with two bars for salty and fresh, which shows how little is fresh, and a second graph of only the fresh water with three bars. One graph cannot do it because the fresh portions would be too small to see beside the ocean bar. (5-ESS2-2)"]},{std:["5-ESS3-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the two reports. Answer out loud.",desc:"combining information about ways communities use science to protect resources and the environment",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two neighborhoods set up a project to cut waste and help each other out.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Neighborhood</th><th>What they did</th><th>Result after one year</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Elm Street</td><td>opened a tool library and a monthly repair session where volunteers mend lamps, bikes, and kettles</td><td>420 items repaired instead of thrown out, and far fewer tools bought that get used twice</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Ash Street</td><td>dug a rain garden in a low corner so roof water soaks in instead of running to the storm drain</td><td>street flooding after heavy rain stopped, and the storm drain carries far less grit and oil</td></tr></table>`,qs:["A tool library means fewer tools are made. Name two resources that saves.","A rain garden slows water down. Explain how that keeps grit and oil out of the storm drain.","Combine the two reports into a plan for a third neighborhood with a lot of waste and a flooding corner."],key:["Any two: the metal, plastic, and wood a tool is made from, the energy used to make and ship it, and the landfill space it would take up when thrown away. (5-ESS3-1)","Water held in the garden soaks slowly into the soil, and the soil and plant roots trap the grit and oil the water was carrying rather than letting it rush straight into the drain and on to a stream. (5-ESS3-1)","Set up a tool library and repair session to cut waste, and dig a rain garden in the low corner so roof water soaks in instead of flooding the street and carrying grit to the drain. The plan has to draw on each report. (5-ESS3-1)"]}]},{id:27,title:"Design a Meal Carrier",short:"Design It",parts:[{std:["3-5-ETS1-1"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Listen, then answer out loud.",desc:"defining a design problem with criteria for success and constraints",read:"A group delivers twenty hot meals to neighbors each Friday. By the last stop the food has gone lukewarm, and twice a tray has tipped on the step and spilled. They want something that carries twenty meals, keeps them warm for an hour, and does not let a tray tip. One volunteer must be able to lift it, it has to fit in a car boot, and it can only be built from cardboard, foil, tape, and old blankets, because there is no money for it.",qs:["Say the problem in one sentence.","Name three things the finished carrier must do to count as a success.","Name two limits the group has to work inside."],key:["Meals go lukewarm and trays tip during the delivery round, so the group needs a way to carry twenty meals safely and keep them warm. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Any three criteria from the story: carries twenty meals, keeps them warm for an hour, stops a tray tipping, and can be lifted by one volunteer. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Any two constraints: it must fit in a car boot, and it can only be made from cardboard, foil, tape, and old blankets with no money spent. (3-5-ETS1-1)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing possible solutions against the criteria and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three designs, each tested with twenty trays of hot water at 70 degrees and carried on the usual round.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Design</th><th>Temperature after an hour</th><th>Trays tipped</th><th>One person can lift it?</th><th>Fits a car boot?</th><th>Free materials only?</th></tr>
     <tr><td>E: a stack of open trays in a blanket</td><td>44 degrees</td><td>3 of 20</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>F: a cardboard box lined with foil, with a cardboard slot for each tray</td><td>58 degrees</td><td>0 of 20</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>G: a bought insulated catering case</td><td>66 degrees</td><td>0 of 20</td><td>yes, just</td><td>only one case, not two</td><td>no</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which design kept the meals warmest? Name two reasons it is still not the right choice.","Compare E and F. Name two criteria F meets that E does not. Then say what in the design explains each.","Which design would you recommend, and list the criteria and limits it satisfies."],key:["G stayed at 66 degrees, but it has to be bought, which breaks the free materials limit, and only one case fits the boot, so it cannot carry the whole round. (3-5-ETS1-2)","F keeps the food warmer, 58 degrees against 44, because the foil lining reflects heat back and the closed box holds the warm air in, and F tips no trays because each one sits in its own slot. (3-5-ETS1-2)","F. It carries twenty meals, holds them at 58 degrees after an hour, tipped no trays, one person can lift it, it fits the boot, and it is made from cardboard, foil, and tape. (3-5-ETS1-2)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the test plan. Answer out loud.",desc:"planning a fair test with controlled variables and naming the failure points to improve",html:()=>`<p class="big">The group tested whether a layer of blanket around the foil box helped.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>Run 1</th><th>Run 2</th></tr>
     <tr><td>box</td><td>foil lined, no blanket</td><td>foil lined, wrapped in one old blanket</td></tr>
     <tr><td>trays</td><td>20 trays of water at 70 degrees</td><td>20 trays of water at 70 degrees</td></tr>
     <tr><td>outside temperature</td><td>9 degrees</td><td>9 degrees</td></tr>
     <tr><td>time</td><td>60 min</td><td>60 min</td></tr>
     <tr><td>temperature after</td><td>58 degrees</td><td>64 degrees</td></tr>
     <tr><td>what went wrong</td><td>the top tray was 8 degrees cooler than the rest</td><td>the blanket slipped off the corner every time the box was lifted</td></tr></table>`,qs:["What was held the same, and what was changed? Why does that make the test fair?","Suppose run 2 had used water at 80 degrees instead. Could you still say the blanket caused the better result? Explain.","Name one change that would fix the failure in each run."],key:["The number and starting temperature of the trays, the outside temperature, and the time were all held the same, and only the blanket changed, so the 6 degree difference can be traced to the blanket. (3-5-ETS1-3)","No. Two things would have changed, the blanket and the starting temperature, so a warmer finish could have come from the hotter start. That is not a fair test. (3-5-ETS1-3)","Run 1 lost heat from the top, so add a foil and blanket lid or put the least urgent delivery on top. Run 2's blanket slipped, so tape or stitch it on, or cut a fitted sleeve with handles that pass through it. Any fix tied to the stated failure counts. (3-5-ETS1-3)"]}]},{id:28,title:"Many Nations, Many Languages",short:"Many Nations",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.1"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing the variety among pre-Columbian nations rather than treating them as one group",html:()=>`<p class="big">Before 1492 there were hundreds of separate nations in North America, speaking hundreds of different languages, with their own governments, territories, and ways of life.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Nation</th><th>Region</th><th>Known for</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Hopi</td><td>desert Southwest</td><td>farming corn on dry mesas and villages built of stone and adobe</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Haida</td><td>Pacific Northwest coast</td><td>ocean-going cedar canoes and carved house posts</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Lakota</td><td>Great Plains</td><td>following the buffalo herds across the northern grassland</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Cherokee</td><td>southeastern woodlands</td><td>farming towns, a council system, and later a written alphabet of their own</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name two nations on the chart and say what made their ways of life different from each other.","A book from long ago calls everyone on this chart by one single name. Explain what is wrong with that.","Hundreds of languages were spoken. What does that suggest about how long people had been living here and how separately?","All four of these nations still exist today. Why is it important to say that in the present tense?"],key:["Any supported pair. For example the Hopi farmed corn in a dry place and built permanent stone and adobe villages, while the Lakota followed buffalo herds and used homes they could carry. Partial: two nations named with no difference between them is approaching. (HSS-5.1)","It treats hundreds of separate nations with different languages, governments, territories, and ways of living as if they were one people. That is as wrong as calling everyone in Europe by one name. Partial: saying the name is rude with no account of the many separate nations is approaching. (HSS-5.1)","That people had lived here for a very long time, long enough for languages to grow apart into entirely separate families, and that many nations developed on their own in their own territories. Partial: one of the long time and the separate development named is approaching. (HSS-5.1)","Because these are living nations with members, governments, and territories today, not only people from the past. Talking about them only in the past tense suggests they disappeared, which is not true. Partial: saying they are still here with nothing said about governments or territories today is approaching. (HSS-5.1)"]}]},{id:29,title:"What Crossed the Ocean",short:"Exchange",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.2"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing what the early explorations carried in both directions and the effects of that exchange",html:()=>`<p class="big">The voyages after 1492 moved plants, animals, and illnesses between continents that had been separate for thousands of years.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>From the Americas to Europe, Africa, and Asia</th><th>From Europe and Africa to the Americas</th></tr>
     <tr><td>potatoes, maize, tomatoes, chilli peppers, cacao, vanilla, beans, squash</td><td>wheat, rice, sugar cane, coffee, bananas, apples</td></tr>
     <tr><td>turkeys</td><td>horses, cattle, pigs, sheep, chickens</td></tr>
     <tr><td>silver and gold taken from mines</td><td>smallpox, measles, and other illnesses never known there before</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name two foods eaten widely in Europe today that came from the Americas.","Horses reached the Great Plains and changed how nations there hunted and traveled. Explain how one animal could do that.","The last row on the right lists illnesses. Explain why their arrival had such a terrible effect.","Was this exchange equal? Give a reason for your answer using the chart."],key:["Any two from the left column: potatoes, maize, tomatoes, chilli peppers, cacao for chocolate, vanilla, beans, or squash. Partial: one food named is approaching. (HSS-5.2)","A hunter on horseback could follow and reach buffalo herds that were impossible to catch on foot, carry far more, and move camp much further and faster. Within a few generations it reshaped daily life, hunting, and travel on the Plains. Partial: one change named is approaching. (HSS-5.2)","Nobody in the Americas had ever been exposed to them, so there was no built-up defense, and whole communities fell ill at once with no one well enough to care for the sick. Enormous numbers of people died, in many places before they ever met a European. Partial: saying people had no medicine is not the point; naming the lack of built-up defense with nothing said about the scale is approaching. (HSS-5.2)","No. Food crops moved both ways, but the deadly illnesses moved almost entirely one way, and silver and gold were taken out of the Americas. The losses fell overwhelmingly on the peoples already living here. Partial: saying no with no reason from the chart is approaching. (HSS-5.2)"]},{std:["HSS-5.3"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining the part interpreters and go-betweens played in cooperation and conflict",html:()=>`<p class="big">Neither side spoke the other's language, so a few people who spoke both held real power.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Person</th><th>What they did</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Tisquantum, often called Squanto</td><td>had been taken to Europe and returned speaking English; interpreted between Plymouth and the Wampanoag</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Sacagawea</td><td>traveled with Lewis and Clark, interpreted with Shoshone people, and helped the party get horses</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Haudenosaunee speakers at treaty councils</td><td>held the agreement in memory and recited it aloud so everyone present knew its terms</td></tr>
     <tr><td>colonial interpreters hired by a colony</td><td>paid by one side, and sometimes chosen for being willing to translate loosely</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Why would a person who spoke both languages have more influence than their job title suggests?","The last row says an interpreter was paid by one side. Explain the problem that creates.","Haudenosaunee speakers recited an agreement aloud at councils. Name one advantage of that over a filed paper.","Suppose you were a leader making an agreement and could not check the translation yourself. Name one thing you would do to protect yourself."],key:["Every word each side heard passed through them, so they could soften, sharpen, or leave out whatever they chose, and nobody else could tell. The agreement people thought they had made was whatever the interpreter said it was. Partial: saying they could translate with nothing said about what they could change is approaching. (HSS-5.3)","An interpreter paid by one side has a reason to favor that side, so the other side cannot trust that it is hearing what was actually said. Partial: saying it is unfair with no account of the reason to favor one side is approaching. (HSS-5.3)","Everyone present hears the terms at the same time and can object on the spot, and the agreement stays in many people's memory rather than in one locked drawer controlled by one side. Partial: saying it is easier to remember with nothing said about witnesses or objecting on the spot is approaching. (HSS-5.3)","Any sensible answer: bring your own interpreter as well, have the terms repeated aloud in front of many witnesses, insist on a record in your own form such as a belt, or agree only to terms simple enough to check. Partial: saying you would not trust them with no step you would actually take is approaching. (HSS-5.3)"]}]},{id:30,title:"Slavery in the Colonies",short:"Colonies",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.4"],n:4,dur:185,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing slavery as a colonial institution and the lives and resistance of enslaved and free Black people",html:()=>`<p class="big">Slavery existed in every one of the thirteen colonies, though the numbers and the work differed greatly from region to region.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Where</th><th>What enslaved people were made to do</th></tr>
     <tr><td>southern colonies</td><td>field work on tobacco, rice, and indigo plantations, and skilled trades on the estate</td></tr>
     <tr><td>middle colonies</td><td>farm work, dock and warehouse work, and household work in towns</td></tr>
     <tr><td>New England</td><td>household work, trades, and work in shipping and on the docks</td></tr></table>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>What the laws did</th><th>What people did in response</th></tr>
     <tr><td>made enslavement last for life and pass to children</td><td>kept families, names, music, and skills alive across generations</td></tr>
     <tr><td>in many colonies, made it illegal to teach an enslaved person to read</td><td>learned to read anyway, in secret, and taught others</td></tr>
     <tr><td>restricted travel, meetings, and the right to testify in court</td><td>escaped, slowed or spoiled work, petitioned courts, and formed their own congregations, the first independent Black religious societies in the colonies, where they could</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Colonial religious institutions stood on both sides of slavery. Many owners required enslaved people to attend services where ministers taught obedience. In the 1750s the Quaker meetings of Pennsylvania began to forbid their own members to buy or hold enslaved people, the earliest organized religious opposition to slavery in the colonies.</p>`,qs:["Slavery existed in all thirteen colonies. Why did the work look so different in New England than in South Carolina?","Name two things the laws were designed to prevent, and say what each one tells you about what lawmakers feared.","Give two examples of enslaved people acting to resist their situation.","According to the note, religious institutions stood on both sides of slavery. Name what each side did. Then say why it matters to describe what enslaved people did and not only what was done to them."],key:["The economy differed. Large plantation crops in the south needed enormous numbers of field workers, while New England's economy was shipping, trades, and small farms, so enslaved people there were used in households, trades, and on the docks in much smaller numbers. Partial: one of the two regions explained is approaching. (HSS-5.4)","Any two: reading, because a person who reads can gather information, write a pass, and reach others; meeting together, because organizing needs meetings; and travel and testimony, because both would give a person a way out or a voice. The laws show lawmakers feared exactly those things. Partial: two things named with nothing said about what lawmakers feared is approaching. (HSS-5.4)","Any two from the chart: learning to read in secret and teaching others, escaping, slowing or spoiling work, petitioning courts, keeping families and culture alive, and forming their own congregations. Partial: one example named is approaching. (HSS-5.4)","One side: many owners required attendance at services where ministers taught obedience, so a religious institution was used to hold the system in place. The other side: from the 1750s the Quaker meetings of Pennsylvania forbade their own members to buy or hold enslaved people, the earliest organized religious opposition to slavery in the colonies. Credit as well the enslaved people who formed their own congregations, which is a third use of the same kind of institution. On the second half: people were not only victims, they made choices, took risks, built communities and kept their families and culture alive under terrible conditions, and leaving that out tells the story wrongly. Both halves are needed for full credit. Partial: one half without the other is approaching, so both sides of the religious institutions with nothing on the second half sits there. (HSS-5.4)"]},{std:["HSS-5.5"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the two accounts. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing two accounts of the same event and explaining how it became a cause of the Revolution",html:()=>`<p class="big">On a March evening in 1770 a crowd gathered around British soldiers guarding a building in Boston. Shouting and thrown objects were followed by soldiers firing, and five colonists died. Both sides then published their version.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>The colonial account</th><th>The soldiers' account</th></tr>
     <tr><td>peaceful townspeople were shot down without warning</td><td>a large crowd pressed in, threw objects, and struck at the guards</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a printed engraving showed soldiers firing in a neat line on an officer's order</td><td>the soldiers said no order was given and they fired in confusion</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the event was named a massacre and the engraving was reprinted everywhere</td><td>at the trial most of the soldiers were found not guilty</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name one point the two accounts agree on and one point where they contradict each other.","The printed engraving was reprinted all over the colonies. Explain why a picture could matter more than the trial verdict.","Why would each side want its own account believed?","A historian has both accounts and the trial record. What should the historian do with them?"],key:["They agree that a crowd gathered, that soldiers fired, and that colonists died. They contradict each other on whether the crowd was peaceful or threatening and on whether an order to fire was given. Partial: one of the agreement and the contradiction named is approaching. (HSS-5.5)","Far more people saw the engraving than read the trial record, and a picture is quick, memorable, and emotional. It shaped what most colonists believed had happened, which is what mattered politically. Partial: saying pictures are powerful with no account of how many people saw it is approaching. (HSS-5.5)","Colonial leaders wanted proof that British soldiers in colonial towns were a danger, which supported their case against quartering and against British rule. The army wanted to show its soldiers had been attacked and acted lawfully. Partial: one side's reason given is approaching. (HSS-5.5)","Compare them, note where they agree and where they differ, ask who wrote each one and what they wanted from it, and be honest about what cannot be settled from the evidence. Partial: saying the historian should pick the true one is the misreading to watch for and is approaching. (HSS-5.5)"]},{std:["HSS-5.6"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing the home front during the Revolution and its part in the course of the war",html:()=>`<p class="big">The war reached into every town, not only the battlefields.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>At home</th><th>What it meant</th></tr>
     <tr><td>boycotts of British goods</td><td>no imported cloth, tea, glass, or paper, so families made do or made their own</td></tr>
     <tr><td>spinning and weaving at home</td><td>women organized public spinning days, and homespun cloth became a sign of support</td></tr>
     <tr><td>running farms and businesses</td><td>women ran the farm, shop, or trade alone for years and wrote letters advising husbands away at the war</td></tr>
     <tr><td>paper money losing its value</td><td>prices rose fast, soldiers' pay bought less and less, and supplying the army got harder</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Explain how a boycott at home affected the fighting.","Homespun cloth became a sign of support. How can a piece of cloth carry a political meaning?","Paper money lost its value and prices rose. Name two groups that hurt, and say how.","Pick one row and explain why the war could not have been sustained without it."],key:["It cut British trade, which put pressure on Parliament through merchants, and it meant the colonies had to supply themselves, which built up local making and reduced dependence on Britain during the war. Partial: one of the two effects named is approaching. (HSS-5.6)","Wearing homespun showed everyone you were keeping the boycott, and imported cloth showed you were not. The cloth itself announced which side you were on. Partial: saying it was patriotic with no account of what the cloth announced is approaching. (HSS-5.6)","Any two: soldiers, whose pay bought less and less; families buying food at rising prices; and the army and Congress, who could not buy supplies as the money they offered lost value. Partial: one group named with how it hurt is approaching. (HSS-5.6)","Any supported answer. For example without women running farms, shops, and trades there would have been no food, no supplies, and no income while so many men were away for years at a time. Partial: a row named with no account of why the war needed it is approaching. (HSS-5.6)"]}]},{id:31,title:"Rights and Growing Cities",short:"New Nation",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.7"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining the rights in the First Amendment and how they work in everyday situations",html:()=>`<p class="big">The First Amendment protects five freedoms from being taken away by the government.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Freedom</th><th>What it protects</th></tr>
     <tr><td>belief</td><td>the government may not set up an official religion or stop you following your own</td></tr>
     <tr><td>speech</td><td>you may say what you think, including criticising the government</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the press</td><td>newspapers and reporters may publish without government permission</td></tr>
     <tr><td>assembly</td><td>people may gather peacefully</td></tr>
     <tr><td>petition</td><td>people may ask the government to put something right</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which freedom protects a newspaper that prints a story criticising the mayor?","A group wants to gather peacefully outside the town hall with signs. Which two freedoms are at work?","Why would the writers protect the right to criticize the government, rather than only the right to agree with it?","The First Amendment limits the government. Explain why that word matters when people argue about what it covers."],key:["Freedom of the press, and freedom of speech supports it too. (HSS-5.7)","Assembly, because they are gathering peacefully, and speech, because the signs carry what they want to say. Petition applies as well if they are asking the government to change something. Partial: one of the two freedoms named is approaching. (HSS-5.7)","A government that can punish criticism can hide its mistakes and stay in power regardless of what people think. Protecting criticism is what lets people find out what their government is doing and change it. Partial: saying people should be allowed to complain with no account of what criticism makes possible is approaching. (HSS-5.7)","Because it tells you who is restrained. It stops the government silencing you; it does not require a private club, a shop, or a website to carry whatever anyone wants to say. Most arguments turn on that distinction. Partial: saying it protects free speech everywhere is the misreading to watch for and is approaching. (HSS-5.7)"]},{std:["HSS-5.8"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining the growth of northeastern cities and factory towns before 1860",html:()=>`<p class="big">In the first half of the 1800s the Northeast filled with mill towns and its cities grew very fast.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Fact</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
     <tr><td>why mills went where they did</td><td>fast-falling rivers turned water wheels, so mills were built at falls and rapids</td></tr>
     <tr><td>who worked in the early cloth mills</td><td>young women recruited from farms, living in company boarding houses, and later immigrant families</td></tr>
     <tr><td>New York City population</td><td>about 60,000 in 1800 and over 800,000 by 1860</td></tr>
     <tr><td>what the growth brought</td><td>work and wages, and also crowded housing, bad water, and disease</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("bridge",70)}${F.icon("truck",70)}${F.icon("drop",70)}</div>`,qs:["Why were the first cloth mills built beside fast-falling rivers?","Young women left farms to work in the mills. Name one reason that was attractive and one reason it was hard.","New York grew more than thirteen times over in sixty years. Name two things a city has to build fast when that happens.","The last row lists good and bad together. Explain why a historian would refuse to describe this growth as simply good or simply bad."],key:["The falling water turned the water wheels that drove the machines. Before steam power was common, the river was the engine, so the mill had to be where the drop was. Partial: naming the water with nothing said about driving the machines is approaching. (HSS-5.8)","Attractive because it was paid work of their own, often the first wages they had ever earned, and a way off the family farm. Hard because the hours were very long, the work was noisy and dangerous, and life in the boarding house was closely controlled. Partial: one of the attractive and the hard given is approaching. (HSS-5.8)","Any two: housing, clean water supply, sewers, streets, markets, schools, and ways of getting food in. The chart's last row shows what happens when they are not built fast enough. Partial: one thing named is approaching. (HSS-5.8)","Because both happened to real people at the same time. The same growth that gave families wages and work also crowded them into housing with bad water where disease spread. A single verdict leaves out half the evidence. Partial: naming one side only, good or bad, is approaching. (HSS-5.8)"]},{std:["HSS-5.9"],n:5,dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud. A map is fine to look at.",desc:"locating mid-Atlantic states and naming their capitals",html:()=>`<div class="figure">${F.icon("map",96)}${F.icon("flag",96)}</div>
     <p class="big">Questions about the middle of the Atlantic coast.</p>`,qs:["Name the states usually counted as the mid-Atlantic.","What is the capital of New Jersey, and what is the capital of Maryland?","Washington, D.C. is the capital of the country but is not a state. Which two states gave up land to create it, and which one still borders it?","What is the capital of Delaware, and which state is the second smallest by area?","How many states are there in all, and name three whose capital is not their biggest city."],key:["New York, New Jersey, Pennsylvania, Delaware, and Maryland. Accept Virginia and West Virginia if the student explains why. Partial: three or four of the five named is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","Trenton, New Jersey and Annapolis, Maryland. Note if the student names Baltimore, which is the usual slip. Partial: one of the two capitals right is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","Maryland and Virginia gave up land, and the Virginia part was later returned. Maryland still borders Washington, D.C. Partial: the two states named with no answer about which still borders it is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","Dover, Delaware. Delaware is the second smallest state by area, after Rhode Island. Partial: one of the two answered is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","50 states. Any three correct pairs, such as New York with Albany, California with Sacramento, Illinois with Springfield, Washington with Olympia, Maryland with Annapolis, or Florida with Tallahassee. Partial: the count right with one or two correct pairs is approaching. (HSS-5.9)"]}]}];const WC=FLU.split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;const readPart=STOPS[3].parts[0];readPart.n=WC;readPart.key[0]=readPart.key[0].replace("__WC__",WC);readPart.desc=`a ${WC}-word grade-level passage read aloud`;const LEVELUPS={2:{id:"L2",title:"Level Up: Cite the Evidence",std:["RI.6.1"],needs:["RI.5.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Cite the exact words, and say what each piece of evidence shows.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>The article separates what the tally sheet records from what it does not. Cite the evidence for both halves, and explain what the writer is arguing by setting them side by side.</li><li>Cite textual evidence to support this analysis: the article treats the 102 items that could not be fixed as a normal result rather than a failure.</li></ol>${artHtml(true)}`,key:["What it records: “The volunteers keep a tally sheet,” the 611 items, the 388, the 102, the 121 and the 44, and the groups 214, 177, and 220. What it does not record: “What the tally sheet does not record is the part the volunteers talk about most, which is that people stay.” Setting them side by side argues that the countable result is not the important one, and that a visit is worth something even when nothing gets mended. (RI.6.1, citing evidence to support analysis)","Evidence: the 102 “could not be fixed at all and were sent home with a note about where to take them.” The note is the point. The writer records the 102 in the same plain sentence as the rest, gives the table a next step for them, and spends no words on blame. Accept any answer that cites the sentence and explains what the citation shows. (RI.6.1, citing evidence to support analysis)"]},3:{id:"L3",title:"Level Up: Powers and Order",std:["6.EE.A.2c"],needs:["5.OA.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Say which step you did first.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Evaluate 3 + 4<sup>2</sup> × 2</li><li>Evaluate 2(x + 5)<sup>2</sup> when x = 1</li><li>A square patch has sides of s meters. Its area is s<sup>2</sup>. Find the area when s = 3/4.</li></ol>`,key:["35. Exponent first: 4² = 16. Then 16 × 2 = 32. Then 3 + 32 = 35. (6.EE.A.2c, order of operations with an exponent)","72. Parentheses first: 1 + 5 = 6. Then the exponent: 6² = 36. Then 2 × 36 = 72. (6.EE.A.2c, evaluating a formula at a given value)","9/16 of a square meter. s² means s × s, so (3/4) × (3/4) = 9/16. (6.EE.A.2c, evaluating an expression with an exponent at a given value)"]},5:{id:"L5",title:"Level Up: Cite the Story",std:["RL.6.1"],needs:["RL.5.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Cite the exact words, and say what each piece of evidence shows.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Cite textual evidence to support this analysis: Ines changes what she wants, and the story shows the change without ever stating it.</li><li>Mr. Wendt says almost nothing in this story. Cite his two lines and explain what the story builds out of them.</li></ol>${F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true)}`,key:["Evidence from the start: she signed up “mostly to get out of a Saturday chess club,” the textiles table is the one “which Ines had been assigned to without being asked,” and electrics is “where everyone wants to sit.” Evidence from the end: “she wrote her name in the textiles column, which was shorter, and slid it back.” The story never says she changed her mind. It puts the two facts at either end and leaves the reader to close the gap. (RL.6.1, citing evidence to support analysis)","His lines are “You will get electrics in March... There is a list” and “March. Electrics. Put your name in.” They are the same offer twice, which turns the clipboard at the end into a test of whether anything has changed. He makes no speech about it, so the whole weight falls on what Ines writes. (RL.6.1, citing evidence to support analysis)"]},6:{id:"L6",title:"Level Up: Divide by a Fraction",std:["6.NS.A.1"],needs:["5.NF.B.4"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Explain what each answer means.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Work out 3/4 ÷ 1/8 and say what the answer counts.</li><li>A spool holds 6 meters of thread. Each repair uses 2/3 of a meter. How many repairs does the spool cover?</li><li>Work out 2/3 ÷ 4/5.</li></ol>`,key:["6. It counts how many eighths fit into three quarters: 3/4 = 6/8, so six 1/8 lengths fit. Multiplying by the reciprocal gives 3/4 × 8/1 = 24/4 = 6. (6.NS.A.1)","9 repairs. 6 ÷ 2/3 = 6 × 3/2 = 18/2 = 9. Check: 9 × 2/3 = 18/3 = 6 meters. (6.NS.A.1)","5/6. Multiply by the reciprocal: 2/3 × 5/4 = 10/12, which reduces to 5/6. (6.NS.A.1)"]},7:{id:"L7",title:"Level Up: Whose Account?",std:["RI.6.9"],needs:["RI.5.9"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Use details from both texts.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Compare how the article and the letter present the same repair table. What does each writer put at the center, and what does each leave out?</li><li>Both texts describe the same forty minute repair in November. Compare the two accounts. What does the difference tell you about each writer's purpose?</li></ol><div class="texts"><article>${srcLine(ART_SRC)}<div class="passage sm">${paras(ART)}</div></article><article>${srcLine(LETTER_SRC)}<div class="passage sm">${paras(LETTER)}</div></article></div>`,key:["The article is a reporter's account built on the tally sheet: counts, groups, and a timed average, with the people appearing only in the last paragraph. The letter is a volunteer's account built on one rule and one repair, with the counts pushed aside in its second sentence. The article leaves out why the rules exist. The letter leaves out the scale, which is the thing the article is best at. (RI.6.9, comparing two authors' presentations of the same events)","The article records the November Saturday as a measurement, “the average visit, timed over one Saturday in November, was 52 minutes.” The letter tells the same month as a scene: “a seven-year-old sewed a bag strap at my table over forty minutes, with one of our newer volunteers threading the needle for her, and neither of us touched the bag.” The reporter's purpose is to describe the table to people who have not been, so she measures. The volunteer's purpose is to defend a rule, so he gives the one case that proves it. (RI.6.9, comparing accounts and purposes)"]},8:{id:"L8",title:"Level Up: Pronouns and Clauses",std:["L.6.1"],needs:["L.5.1"],n:3,dir:"Say each answer out loud.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Which is right, and why? A: Mr. Wendt handed the clipboard to Ines and I. B: Mr. Wendt handed the clipboard to Ines and me.</li><li>Fix the vague pronoun: “Ines told the girl that she had threaded the needle eleven times.”</li><li>Fix the shift in person: “A volunteer should stay at your own table until one o'clock.”</li></ol>`,key:["B. The pronoun follows the preposition to, so it has to be in the objective case: me. Test it by dropping the other name: “handed the clipboard to me,” not “to I.” (L.6.1a and L.6.1b, pronoun case)","Any version that names the person, such as “Ines told the girl that Ines had threaded the needle eleven times,” or better, “Ines said she had threaded the needle eleven times, and the girl laughed.” As written, she could be either of them. (L.6.1d, correcting vague pronouns)","A volunteer should stay at his or her own table until one o'clock. Or make it plural: Volunteers should stay at their own tables until one o'clock. The sentence starts in the third person and must stay there. (L.6.1c, correcting an inappropriate shift in person)"]}};const READ_STD=["RL.5.1","RL.5.4","RI.5.1","RI.5.9","RL.6.1","RI.6.1","RI.6.9"];const WRITE_STD=["W.5.1","W.5.2","W.5.3"];const MATH_STD=["5.OA.A.1","5.NF.B.4","5.NBT.A.1","5.NBT.A.3","5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.3","5.MD.C.3","5.MD.C.5","6.EE.A.2c","6.NS.A.1"];const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0];function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}function strips(){const dens=[1,2,3,4,5,6,8,10,12],W=600,h=32;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W+8} ${dens.length*(h+6)+4}" width="100%" style="max-width:640px" role="img" aria-label="Fraction strips for halves, thirds, fourths, fifths, sixths, eighths, tenths, and twelfths">`;dens.forEach((d,r)=>{for(let k=0;k<d;k++){const x=4+k*W/d,y=2+r*(h+6);s+=`<rect x="${x}" y="${y}" width="${W/d}" height="${h}" fill="${r%2?"var(--sea-soft)":"var(--surface)"}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="${x+W/d/2}" y="${y+h/2}" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" font-size="${d>8?13:16}" fill="var(--ink)">${d===1?"1":"1/"+d}</text>`}});return s+"</svg>"}const orderHtml=()=>`<ol class="starters"><li>Work the innermost grouping symbol first: parentheses ( ), then brackets [ ], then braces { }.</li><li>Inside a grouping, do exponents first, then multiply and divide, then add and subtract.</li><li>Rewrite the whole expression after every step so nothing gets lost.</li><li>Check at the end: did the grouping symbols change the order? If they did not, say so.</li></ol>`;const fmulHtml=()=>`<ul class="starters"><li>Fraction times fraction: multiply the tops, then multiply the bottoms. (3/4) × (2/5) = 6/20.</li><li>A whole number can be written over 1: 5 = 5/1.</li><li>“Of” means multiply: 2/3 of 12 is 2/3 × 12.</li><li>(a/b) × q means: split q into b equal parts, then take a of them.</li><li>Area of a rectangle with fraction sides: multiply the two side lengths. The answer is in square units.</li><li>Tiling: if each tile is 1/10 by 1/10, each tile is worth 1/100 of a square unit.</li><li>Dividing by a fraction: multiply by its reciprocal. 6 ÷ 2/3 = 6 × 3/2.</li></ul>`;const chunkHtml=()=>F.table(["Word part","What it means"],[["un-, in-, ir-, im-","not, or the opposite of"],["re-","again or back"],["pre-","before"],["dis-","apart or not"],["-tion, -ion","the act or result of"],["-able, -ible","can be"],["-ity","the state of being"],["-ance, -ence","the act or state of"],["-ous","full of"],["-ism","a system or a way of working"],["-ship","the state or skill of"],["rep, par","get ready, make ready"],["lia, rely","trust or depend"],["mech","machine"]])+`<p class="small">Cover the prefix and the suffix, read the middle, then put the word back together.</p>`;const tenseHtml=()=>F.table(["Tense or form","How to build it","Example"],[["Past perfect","had + the past participle","The table had run for 26 months."],["Present perfect","has or have + the past participle","Mr. Wendt has sat there for 26 months."],["Future perfect","will have + the past participle","By March she will have worked nine Saturdays."],["Tense shift","keep one time through the whole sentence","She carried and set up, not carried and sets up."],["Correlative pairs","both and, either or, neither nor, not only but also",""],["Interjection","a word that marks a pause or a feeling","Well, that clarinet is beyond us."]])+`<p class="small">With either or and neither nor, the verb matches the subject nearer to it.</p>`;const frames=list=>`<ul class="starters">${list.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;function sentHtml(st){const p=st&&st.parts&&st.parts[0],k=p&&p.alt&&p.alt.key||p&&p.std&&p.std[0]||"RL.5.1";if(k==="opinion"||k==="W.5.1")return frames(["The repair table should ____.","One reason is ____.","The fact box says ____.","Someone on the other side would say ____. However, ____.","For these reasons, ____."]);if(k==="facts"||k==="W.5.2")return frames(["A repair table is ____.","At Westmill, ____.","For example, ____.","The rule about ____ exists because ____.","In short, ____."]);if(k==="story"||k==="W.5.3")return frames(["At first, ____.","“____,” ____ said.","After a while, ____.","By the time ____, ____.","In the end, ____."]);if(k==="W.5.8")return frames(["Note: ____ (Text 1)","Note: ____ (Text 2)","Note: ____ (Text 3)","My sources: 1. ____ by ____. 2. ____. 3. ____ by ____."]);if(k==="RI.5.9"||k==="RI.6.9")return frames(["Text 1 says ____.","Text 2 says ____.","Text 3 says ____.","Put together, ____.","They both use ____, but Text ____ treats it as ____ while Text ____ treats it as ____."]);if(k==="SL.5.2")return frames(["The minutes were about ____.","Three things were decided: ____, ____, and ____.","The vote was ____.","The table shows ____.","Together they tell me ____."]);if(k==="SL.5.4")return frames(["The repair table is ____.","It works like this: ____.","One number from the tally sheet is ____.","If you went, you would see ____.","That is why ____."]);if(k==="RI.5.1"||k==="RI.6.1")return frames(["The article says, “____.”","I can tell ____ because the article says “____.”","That evidence shows ____."]);return frames(["The story says, “____.”","I can tell ____ because the story says “____.”","At the start ____, but by the end ____."])}const TOOLS={chunk:{name:"Prefix, suffix, and root chart",std:["RF.5.3"],html:chunkHtml},mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:MATH_STD,html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const row=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===row&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[row,k];r()})},order:{name:"Order of operations card",std:["5.OA.A.1","6.EE.A.2c"],html:orderHtml},strips:{name:"Fraction strips",std:["5.NF.B.4","5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.3","6.NS.A.1"],html:strips},fmul:{name:"Fraction formula card",std:["5.NF.B.4","5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.3","6.NS.A.1"],html:fmulHtml},tense:{name:"Verb tense and conjunction card",std:["L.5.1","L.6.1"],html:tenseHtml},sent:{name:"Sentence frames",std:WRITE_STD.concat(READ_STD,["W.5.8","SL.5.2","SL.5.4"]),html:sentHtml}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"chunk",std:["RF.5.3"],msg:"The chart gives the meaning of the prefixes, suffixes, and roots, so count the word reading as supported, not independent."},{t:"mult",std:MATH_STD,msg:"The multiplication chart supplies the products. Rate on whether the student sets each problem up correctly and can explain it."},{t:"order",std:["5.OA.A.1","6.EE.A.2c"],msg:"The card gives the order to work the grouping symbols in, and where exponents go, so count these answers as supported, not independent."},{t:"strips",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2"],msg:"Fraction strips show a common size for two unlike fractions, which is the hard half of adding them. Count those answers as supported, not independent."},{t:"fmul",std:["5.NF.B.3"],msg:"The card states that a fraction can be read as a division, which is the whole of this stop. Note it if the card was open."},{t:"strips",std:["5.NF.B.4","6.NS.A.1"],msg:"With fraction strips the student can see the size of each piece. Rate on whether the student can write the equation and explain it."},{t:"fmul",std:["5.NF.B.4","6.NS.A.1"],msg:"The formula card gives the rule for multiplying fractions, the meaning of a fraction of a number, the tiling rule, and the reciprocal rule for dividing. Count this stop as supported, not independent."},{t:"tense",std:["L.5.1","L.6.1"],msg:"The card shows how to build each perfect tense and lists the correlative pairs, so this stop then shows applying a stated rule rather than recall."},{t:"sent",std:WRITE_STD,msg:"Sentence frames supply the opening, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["W.5.8"],msg:"The frames show how to mark a note with its text and how to lay out the source list, so count the source list check as supported."},{t:"sent",std:["RI.5.9","RI.6.9"],msg:"The frames tell the student to draw on each text and name the shape of the comparison, so rate on the content of the answer rather than on the structure."},{t:"sent",std:["RL.5.1","RI.5.1","RL.6.1","RI.6.1"],msg:"The frames remind the student to quote. Rate on the accuracy of the quotation and on what the student says it shows."},{t:"sent",std:["SL.5.2","SL.5.4"],msg:"The frames supply the order of the talk and name the parts of the summary, so count the sequencing check as supported and rate the content."},{t:"passage",std:READ_STD.concat(["W.5.8","RF.5.4"]),msg:"If the text was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading. On the fluency stop there is no supported version: a passage read aloud by an adult cannot be scored for RF.5.4."},{t:"passage",std:["SL.5.2","SL.5.4","W.5.1","W.5.2"],msg:"If you read the table or the fact box aloud, note it. The checks on this stop still stand."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,passageStd:READ_STD.concat(["W.5.8"])}}});
